# Contributing

<!-- Per-repo. Fill in the install/test/lint commands below. Rename to
     CONTRIBUTING.md (drop .example). Delete this comment before
     committing a filled copy. -->

## Development Setup

1. Clone the repository.
2. Install dependencies: `npm install`
3. Run the test suite: `npm test`

## Conventions

- Branch names: `<type>/<kebab-description>` (feat, fix, chore, docs,
  test).
- Commit subjects: `type: description`, imperative mood, 50 characters
  max, no trailing period.
- Name functions with verb-noun pairs (`normalizeUserEmails`, not
  `process`). Provide a return type annotation.
- Name variables for role (`activeUserRecords`, not `d`).
- Comment the why. Document the reasoning; the code shows the
  execution. Do not reference removed code or what changed; git history
  covers that.

## Security and Review

- Never build SQL, shell commands, or evaluated code from untrusted
  input. Use parameterized queries and array-based subprocess calls.
- Get review before destructive changes (dropping tables, deleting
  data) and before adding a new dependency (name, version, purpose).
- Never weaken, skip, or delete a test to force a pass. If a test looks
  wrong, raise it in the pull request instead.
- Keep public APIs backward compatible: accept both old and new
  parameter names on a rename, add new parameters as optional, never
  reorder or remove existing positional parameters.
- Never use MD5 or SHA-1 for passwords, tokens, signatures, or session
  IDs. Use bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2 for passwords, SHA-256 or SHA-3
  elsewhere.
- Never commit secrets, API keys, or credentials. Use environment
  variables or a secret manager.
- Set `persist-credentials: false` on `actions/checkout` steps in
  GitHub Actions workflows unless the job needs the credential
  afterward.
- Docker containers run as non-root by default.
- Never rewrite pushed history on a shared branch. Add new commits
  instead of force-pushing, rebasing, or amending published commits.

## Code Quality

- Check preconditions and validate ranges before use.
- Test for zero before division.
- Avoid nested quantifiers in regular expressions; use atomic groups or
  simpler expressions.
- Never modify a collection while iterating over it.
- Bound recursion with depth limits or visited sets.
- Never log passwords, tokens, or personal data.
- Validate that paths built from untrusted input resolve within the
  target directory.
- Make scripts and migrations safe to re-run.
- Guard shared mutable state with locks or thread-safe structures;
  prefer immutable data and message passing.
- Join, await, or supervise every thread and async task so unhandled
  exceptions surface.
- Keep a consistent lock order to prevent deadlocks, or use a single
  lock.
- Extract nested loops into a helper and return from it, instead of
  breaking out of nested loops.
- Move constant work out of loops, cache compiled regular expressions,
  and use hash lookups over nested iteration.
- Split classes that mix concerns, for example database, transport, and
  UI.
- Never leave a catch block empty. Log context, surface feedback, or
  re-raise.
- Extract magic numbers into named constants.
- Extract repeated sequences into helpers instead of duplicating them.
- Do not leave `TODO`, `FIXME`, or stubbed code in a change. Raise
  incomplete work in the pull request instead.

## Workflow

- Run the project's lint command and fix all errors before opening a
  pull request.
- Never silence a linter or type checker to force a pass; fix the
  cause.
- Update README for substantial changes and CHANGELOG for all changes,
  following Semantic Versioning.

## Making a Change

1. Create a branch following the naming convention above.
2. Make your change. Add or update tests as appropriate.
3. Run the lint command: `npm run lint`
4. Commit following the subject convention above.
5. Open a pull request.

## Reporting Bugs

Open an issue with steps to reproduce, expected behavior, and actual
behavior.
